WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 48: The New Madrid Earthquakes of 1811: The Day the Mississippi Ran Backward

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:07.040
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:17.840
[Rebecca] In the winter of 1811, the American frontier beyond the Appalachian Mountains was a wilderness of ancient forests, river flatboats, and isolated pioneer settlements.

4
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:27.680
[Rebecca] But in the pre-dawn darkness of December 16, 1811, the heart of the North American continent was seized by a cataclysm of unimaginable power.

5
00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:39.040
[Rebecca] Centered beneath the small river outpost of New Madrid in southeastern Missouri, the Earth ruptured in the most violent series of earthquakes in the recorded history of the United States.

6
00:00:39.040 --> 00:01:00.160
[Rebecca] Shaking an area ten times larger than the famous 1906 San Francisco earthquake, the tremors caused the mighty Mississippi River to churn in boiling waves and actually run backward, liquefied entire forested islands, created giant lakes overnight, and rang church bells more than 1,000 miles away on the Atlantic coast.

7
00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.480
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

8
00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:18.720
[George] At 2:15 on the freezing morning of Monday, December 16, 1811, residents of the small trading village of New Madrid in the Missouri Territory were jolted awake by a terrifying, supernatural roar.

9
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:30.800
[George] Contemporaries described the sound not merely as thunder, but as the simultaneous explosion of 10,000 naval cannons accompanied by a low, subterranean groan that shook the marrow of the bone.

10
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:36.560
[George] In seconds, the ground began heaving in colossal rolling waves like an ocean tempest.

11
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.480
[George] Sturdy pioneer log cabins collapsed like houses of cards.

12
00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:53.280
[George] Massive brick chimneys tumbled to earth, and the ground split open in yawning fissures miles long, belching forth geysers of sulfurous black sand, water, and prehistoric coal 60 feet into the air.

13
00:01:53.280 --> 00:02:02.440
[George] Terrified families scrambled into the pitch-black night in their nightclothes, clawing through snow and freezing mud as the forest around them was torn apart.

14
00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:12.360
[George] Ancient white oak and walnut trees whipped wildly against each other, interlocking their branches before being snapped like dry kindling by subterranean shockwaves.

15
00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:25.000
[George] In the early morning hours of December 16, 1811, the quiet frontier village of New Madrid, Missouri, was awakened by an unearthly subterranean roar resembling continuous thunder.

16
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:35.080
[George] Flocks of wild passenger pigeons and waterfowl flew erratically in blind panic as subterranean rumblings shook the timberlands for dozens of miles along the river.

17
00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:47.160
[George] Unlike California earthquakes, which occur along the visible surface boundary between tectonic plates along the San Andreas Fault, the New Madrid earthquakes were an intraplate seismic phenomenon.

18
00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:58.680
[George] Hundreds of millions of years earlier, the North American continent had attempted to rip itself apart, forming an ancient subterranean scar deep in the Earth's crust known as the Realfoot Rift.

19
00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:07.080
[George] Over eons, this deep rift was buried under thousands of feet of dense, unbroken sedimentary rock and alluvial river mud.

20
00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:20.440
[George] Because the continental crust in the eastern United States is far older, colder, and harder than the fractured rock of the Pacific coast, seismic shockwaves travel through it with extraordinary, unbroken efficiency.

21
00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:34.280
[George] Modern geologists estimate that the three primary earthquakes, striking on December 16, January 23, and February 7, reached catastrophic magnitudes between 7.5 and 8.2.

22
00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:50.560
[George] The shockwaves were felt strongly over an astounding one million square miles, awakening President James Madison in the White House in Washington, D.C., cracking plaster in Charleston, South Carolina, and ringing church bells in Boston, Massachusetts.

23
00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.040
[George] More than 1,100 miles away.

24
00:03:53.040 --> 00:04:03.120
[George] The shockwaves originated in the Realfoot Rift, an ancient tectonic fault zone buried thousands of feet beneath the thick, soft sediments of the Mississippi River basin.

25
00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:13.360
[George] Dense clouds of dark sulfur gas hissed from gaping fissures in the clay bluffs, poisoning local wells and forcing frontier families to abandon their homesteads in terror.

26
00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:19.840
[George] The most spectacular and terrifying manifestation of the earthquake occurred along the mighty Mississippi River.

27
00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:34.000
[George] At the time, the river was the primary commercial highway of the West, navigated by hundreds of wooden flatboats, keelboats, and the newly invented experimental steamboat New Orleans, commanded by Captain Nicholas Roosevelt.

28
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:45.520
[George] When the tremors struck, the river bed heaved upward in violent geological thrusts, creating massive temporary waterfalls and rapids where calm river channels had existed moments before.

29
00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:56.480
[George] Giant forested islands, some measuring miles in length, liquefied in seconds and sank completely beneath the churning brown water, drowning entire pioneer encampments.

30
00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:04.800
[George] High mud bluffs along the riverbanks collapsed with colossal roars, generating giant river tsunamis that washed over the shores.

31
00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:21.120
[George] Most astoundingly, the upward thrust of the riverbed at Tiptonville created an immense geological dam that forced the mighty current of the Mississippi River to reverse its direction, flowing northward and backward for several hours, filling newly created subterranean basins.

32
00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:32.760
[George] Giant fissures opened in the earth, swallowing cattle and trees, while geysers of sulphurous sand, coal and boiling water erupted thirty feet into the winter air across millions of acres.

33
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:43.080
[George] Flatboat crews navigating the Mississippi were tossed by violent tidal waves that snapped anchor cables and smashed wooden keelboats against uprooted cottonwood snags.

34
00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:47.400
[George] The geological reshaping of the landscape was profound and permanent.

35
00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:57.720
[George] During the colossal February 7th tremor, the most violent of the sequence, known as the Hard Shock, a massive subsidence occurred across northwestern Tennessee.

36
00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:05.000
[George] An entire 30,000-acre ancient cypress forest dropped anywhere from five to twenty feet in a matter of hours.

37
00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:16.040
[George] As the Mississippi River surged over its collapsed banks, floodwaters poured into the vast sunken depression, completely submerging the drowned forest and creating Realfoot Lake.

38
00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:31.480
[George] To this day, the majestic lake in western Tennessee remains an eerie, beautiful monument to the 1811 cataclysm, where boaters navigate among the fossilized, submerged cypress knees that were dropped below the water table in a single terrifying night.

39
00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:43.640
[George] Near Point Pleasant, the riverbed buckled upward along tectonic faults, creating temporary waterfalls that forced the current of the mighty Mississippi River to flow violently backward for several hours.

40
00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:44.760
[George] In syncent.

41
00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:55.160
[George] Louis and Louisville, chimneys crashed into the streets, plaster fell from ceilings, and church bells rang spontaneously as shockwaves rolled across the continent.

42
00:06:55.160 --> 00:07:06.200
[George] Across thousands of square miles of southeastern Missouri, northeastern Arkansas, and western Kentucky, the rich alluvial earth underwent catastrophic soil liquefaction.

43
00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:17.200
[George] Under the intense, rhythmic vibration of the seismic waves, water saturated sand deep beneath the soil transformed instantly from a solid into a boiling, pressurized liquid.

44
00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:27.920
[George] The compressed slurry burst through the surface in thousands of explosive sand geysers, creating vast fields of crater-like formations known as sandblows or sand boils.

45
00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:40.560
[George] In some areas, white sand and coal dust covered fertile topsoil up to six feet deep, turning thousands of acres of productive agricultural land into barren deserts that could not be ploughed for decades.

46
00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:53.840
[George] The air for fifty miles was filled with a thick, suffocating stench of sulfur and hydrogen sulfide, prompting many devout frontersmen to believe that the gates of hell had opened and that the biblical day of judgment had arrived.

47
00:07:53.840 --> 00:08:05.840
[George] A colossal depression sank more than ten feet into the forest floor, quickly filling with river water to create Realfoot Lake in western Tennessee, drowning virgin cypress forests beneath the waves.

48
00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:11.840
[George] The cosmic scale of the earthquakes resonated deeply within Native American history and culture.

49
00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:25.280
[George] At the time, the great Shawnee warrior and statesman Tecumseh was traveling across the South, striving to unite the Creek, Choctaw, and Cherokee nations into a grand confederacy to resist white American expansion.

50
00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:38.560
[George] Frontier folklore recounts that when skeptical Creek chiefs in Alabama refused his alliance in the autumn of 1811, Tecumseh angrily warned them When I return to Detroit, I will stamp my foot on the ground.

51
00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:40.960
[George] And shake every house in your village.

52
00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:51.280
[George] When the catastrophic New Madrid earthquakes struck weeks later, destroying creek lodges and turning rivers backward, terrified warriors remembered Tecumseh's words.

53
00:08:51.280 --> 00:09:05.720
[George] Believing that the Great Spirit had sent a supernatural sign of support for Tecumseh's cause, hundreds of Southern warriors rushed to join the militant Red Stick faction, sparking the Creek War that became an integral chapter of the War of 1812.

54
00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:19.160
[George] The earthquake shook church bells in Boston, cracked sidewalks in Washington, D.C., and was felt over one million square miles, making it the most geographically widespread seismic event in American history.

55
00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:30.200
[George] Miraculously, because the frontier Mississippi Valley was sparsely populated in 1811, the direct human death toll was estimated in the hundreds rather than the tens of thousands.

56
00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:36.760
[George] In 1815, Congress passed the first federal disaster relief legislation in American history.

57
00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:46.920
[George] The New Madrid Relief Act, allowing pioneer victims whose lands had been ruined by fissures and sandblows to exchange their damaged land for new parcels in the public domain.

58
00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:53.960
[George] Today, the New Madrid seismic zone remains a sleeping geological giant buried beneath the heart of America.

59
00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:57.160
[George] Deep beneath the modern cities of Memphis, St.

60
00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:05.960
[George] Louis, and thousands of bridges, highways, and natural gas pipelines, the ancient rift continues to groan with tectonic pressure.

61
00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:18.760
[George] The New Madrid earthquakes of 1811 stand as an awe-inspiring reminder of the raw, untamed forces that slumber beneath our feet, forces capable of reshaping the geography of a continent in the blink of an eye.

62
00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:31.800
[George] Today, modern seismologists monitor the New Madrid seismic zone with sensitive digital networks, aware that the ancient midcontinental fault remains a sleeping giant capable of waking at any time.

63
00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:37.960
[Rebecca] The New Madrid earthquakes of 1811 remind us of the immense, silent power of our planet.

64
00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:44.960
[Rebecca] It proves that human history is written upon a dynamic, living earth that can shake our greatest certainties in a matter of seconds.

65
00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:47.840
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

66
00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:48.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

67
00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:55.760
[Rebecca] Join us next Tuesday as we follow the dramatic rise and fall of the legendary Missouri outlaw, Jesse James.

68
00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:58.320
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

